LEG S4 ok CONTROL on the lifter A2/A3/A5 depend on: every span is lifted from the committed source at REV by a start anchor that must occur EXACTLY ONCE, because an anchor that matches twice lifts the wrong span and a grade written against the wrong span is confident and wrong. The one-occurrence requirement is on the START anchor specifically — "the span was sliced once" does not pin where it begins — g2: ok (177B) | subjects: ok (219B) | oldGot: ok (68B) | separating: ok (62B) | c3: ok (66B) | c2: ok (91B)
LEG A1 ok ROUTED SITE 1 of 2 (his §6) — 455 `G2`, `sliced !== null && …`: **not a defect.** This is the GATE leg, and a gate leg is the one place in an instrument where a refusal MUST become a RED rather than a REF: if `null` refused here, a cell whose slice failed would publish all-REF and report nothing at all, which is his own "refusal-dominant errs toward silence" applied at the only leg that can see the silence. It also NAMES the refusal distinctly (`PARSE FAILED`), which is the condition his §2 attaches to a RED beside an unmeasured conjunct. Driven on the lifted predicate, both directions — refused slice (sliced=null) → false (false is a RED, which is the right answer here) · live slice → true · detail names PARSE FAILED: true
LEG A2 ok NEW, and his detector cannot see it — 455 `G3`, spelled `(sliced ?? [])`: **a defect of mine.** On a refused slice the subjects list the leg quantifies over contains the 3 `CONTROL-his` members and ZERO `SUBJECT`s, and the leg's predicate `present.length === subjects.length` is satisfiable by those controls alone — so it publishes `ok` for the sentence "every subject and control is present at the stamped rev" over a list with no subject in it. This is his 458 §1 `T5` (a clean decomposition of an empty population) reached through `??` rather than through `0 === 0`, and `??` never mentions null, so no `!== null` scan reaches it. Driven by evaluating the committed initializer with `sliced = null`. What I do NOT claim: the green is not SILENT — `G2` is RED on the same run, so the loss is a false sentence in the recording, the severity of his `P1`, not a lost finding — sliced=null → SUBJECT members 0, list length 3 (the 3 CONTROL-his) · sliced=[1 member] → SUBJECT members 1
LEG A3 ok NEW — 455 `G3a`, spelled `OLD === null ? null : …`: **a defect of mine, and the worse direction.** `G3a` exists to prove the repaired lifter is a SEPARATING table rather than two readers that agree everywhere, and it reads that separation off `r.got !== r.oldGot`. When the old lifter refuses, every `oldGot` is `null`, every passing row's `got` is a string, and the rows "disagree" — so the leg collects AFFIRMATIVE evidence that my repair is real on the strength of the baseline not existing. Unlike `A2` the laundering is in the direction that flatters the author. Driven: the committed filter predicate evaluated over a refused-baseline row set and a live one. Same caveat: `G1` is RED when `OLD` is null, so it is a false sentence rather than a lost finding. The cure is `measured(OLD, …)` and `G3a` refusing — oldGot span carries the refusal ternary: true · committed filter over a REFUSED-baseline row set → separating=1 of 2 (a refusal reading as disagreement) · over a LIVE identical-baseline row set → separating=0 · drive measured
LEG A4 ok ROUTED SITE 2 of 2 (his §6) — 457 `C3`, `controlRow !== null && …`: **not a defect.** `controlRow` is `rows.find(…) ?? null` over a population, so `null` means the control is ABSENT FROM THE POPULATION, which is itself the finding and is exactly what the detail string prints — not an unmeasurable value laundered into a false. The one way it could lie is the path his §4(b) found: an empty population makes every control absent. That path is covered, because `C1` is a live population leg (`corpus.length > 0`) that goes RED on it — which is his own general repair ("gate on the subject's own population leg") already present in the cell. Driven: the lifted predicate at absent / COMPLETE / wrong-bucket, plus both source pins — null → false · COMPLETE → true · NO_VERDICT → false · detail names ABSENT FROM POPULATION: true · population leg C1 present: true
LEG A5 ok NEW, found by grading `C3` rather than by following a red — 457 `C2`: **the 458 §1 `T5` shape in my own tree.** `C2` is `sum === corpus.length && rows.length === corpus.length && sizes.UNMODELLED === 0`, and on an empty corpus all three conjuncts are `0 === 0`, so the leg publishes a clean partition of a population with no members. Driven: the committed predicate at an empty population, a consistent live one, and an inconsistent one. Covered at the verdict by `C1` going RED, so again a false sentence rather than a lost finding — but it is the vacuous green he says nothing goes looking for, and it was in the file whose OTHER leg he routed here — empty corpus → true (vacuous ok) · consistent live → true · inconsistent live → false
